These are all the shell scripts I carry around with myself so I can have a
consistant user experience

Machine context (local, per-machine config that must NOT be shared):
Anything unique to one machine — work paths, credentials, a machine's git
email, machine-only tools — is "machine context" (see CONTEXT.md) and never
goes in this repo. Instead:
  - shells:  put it in ~/.zshrc.local / ~/.bashrc.local (sourced automatically)
  - git:     put it in ~/.gitconfig.local (included automatically)
Templates with the expected fields live in examples/ (not linked by stow).
Copy the relevant examples/*.local.example to the matching ~/.*.local.

iTerm2 (macOS):
Preferences are managed as a scrubbed snapshot at
macos/macos_app_settings/com.googlecode.iterm2.plist. setup_macos_env.sh seeds
it into ~/Library/Preferences on a fresh machine (never overwriting an existing
local file) and enables window/session restoration on reopen.
To refresh the snapshot after changing settings in the iTerm GUI, run:
  ./capture_iterm2_prefs.sh
then commit the updated plist. The script strips machine-local NoSync* keys and
absolute /Users paths, so no machine context is committed.